+7.  **Predict Color:**
+    Compute the mean of the pixel-aligned values across views: $\bar{v}_p = \frac{1}{C} \sum_{c=1}^{C} v(p^x_{c,t})$.
+    Apply positional encoding $\gamma_d(d)$ to the viewing direction $d$ (using $2 \times l$ basis functions as in [28]).
+    Concatenate the mean pixel-aligned value and the encoded direction, then pass through $MLP_{rgb}$ (two linear layers):
+    $$ \tilde{r}^g_{bt}(x) = MLP_{rgb} \left( [\bar{v}_p; \gamma_d(d)] \right) $$
+    Note: The source formula shows $MLP_{rgb}$ taking the mean of values and the direction encoding [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Parameters:**
-*   $\lambda_\theta, \lambda_t$: Regularization weights for pose refinement [S1, unofficial].
-*   $d$: Feature dimension [S2, unofficial].
-*   $l$: Half the number of basis functions in positional encoding [S2, unofficial].
+*   $d$: Feature dimension (Not given in the sources).
+*   $C$: Number of input views.
+*   $l$: Number of frequency bands for directional encoding (Not given in the sources).
 
 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Camera Calibration:** The method assumes relative camera position and orientation are unknown or uncalibrated, relying on relative rotations for averaging [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Projection Errors:** Direct $\mathbb{R}^3 \rightarrow \mathbb{R}^2$ projection may fail if the query point is not visible in a view; handling of occluded views is not explicitly detailed in the provided notes [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Sparse Convolution:** Requires efficient implementation of SparseConvNet for trilinear interpolation in SMPL space [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Occlusion:** If a query point is occluded in some views, the pixel-aligned features $p^x_{c,t}$ may be noisy. The attention mechanism mitigates this by down-weighting incompatible views, but extreme occlusion may require robust skeletal priors.
+*   **SMPL Accuracy:** The skeletal feature retrieval depends on the accuracy of the SMPL pose estimation. Errors in SMPL pose will propagate to $s^0_{c,t}$, potentially misleading the attention weights [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Trilinear Interpolation:** Sampling from the SparseConvNet volume requires careful handling of boundaries outside the SMPL mesh. [G] Points outside the SMPL volume should be handled with a fallback or clamping to avoid NaNs.
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [SparseConvNet-based Skeletal Feature Sampling](16444-sparseconvnet-based-skeletal-feature-sampling.md)
-Samples time-augmented skeletal features at arbitrary 3D query points by diffusing discrete skeletal features into a continuous 3D volume using sparse convolutions. It differs from dense voxelization by processing only occupied voxels, improving efficiency for large 3D spaces. Choose this when memory efficiency for large scenes is critical.
+This method samples time-augmented skeletal features at arbitrary 3D query points by diffusing discrete skeletal features into a continuous 3D volume using sparse convolutions. It differs from dense voxelization by processing only occupied voxels, significantly improving efficiency for large 3D spaces. It is chosen when computational efficiency is critical and the scene contains large empty regions. See [SparseConvNet-based Skeletal Feature Sampling](16444-sparseconvnet-based-skeletal-feature-sampling.md).
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of views $C$, the number of joints $L$, and the feature dimension $d$. The cross-attention mechanism involves matrix multiplications of size $C \times C$ and $C \times d$, making it $O(C^2 d + C d^2)$ per query point.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing features for all views and joints. Sparse convolutions reduce memory usage compared to dense voxelization [S2, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** Improves pose refinement without camera calibration; effectively combines geometric (skeletal) and appearance (image) features; robust to multi-view inconsistencies via attention [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Limitations:** Requires accurate skeletal pose estimation; sensitive to projection errors if views are occluded; computational cost increases with the number of views [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **When to Use:** Multi-view human performance rendering where camera calibration is unavailable or unreliable.
-*   **When Not to Use:** Single-view scenarios; scenes without articulated human subjects.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the multi-view attention mechanism, which is $O(C^2 \cdot d)$ for the attention weights and $O(C \cdot d)$ for the aggregation, plus the MLP inference. The SparseConvNet sampling is efficient due to sparsity.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the SparseConvNet volume for skeletal features and the image features for all views. The attention matrix is $C \times C$.
+*   **Strengths:** Effectively combines geometric priors (SMPL) with visual details (images). Handles multi-view inconsistencies via attention. Improves rendering quality for articulated humans.
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on accurate SMPL pose estimation. Computationally more expensive than standard NeRF due to multi-view processing and attention.
+*   **When to use:** Dynamic human rendering where multi-view consistency and geometric priors are beneficial.
+*   **When not to use:** Static scenes without articulated subjects, or resource-constrained environments where SMPL inference and multi-view attention are too costly.
 
 ## Background
-Originated in the context of Articulated Neural Radiance Fields (A-NeRF) and Neural Human Performer. A-NeRF introduced multi-view constraints for pose refinement using relative rotations [S1, unofficial]. Neural Human Performer extended this by aggregating skeletal and pixel-aligned features via cross-attention for generalizable radiance fields [S2, unofficial].
+The method originates from the "Neural Human Performer" paper, which introduces a framework for learning generalizable radiance fields for human performance rendering. It builds on the idea of using skeletal priors (SMPL) to regularize the radiance field, extending it with multi-view aggregation to handle dynamic scenes and multiple viewpoints [S1, unofficial]. The multi-view pose refinement variant is described in "A-NeRF," which focuses on articulated neural radiance fields for learning human shape, appearance, and pose, incorporating multi-view constraints for pose refinement [S2, unofficial].
 
